### Problem
1. The system power manager references the wiring PMIC and FuelGauge driver, which will construct the `Wire` instance and initialize the TX/RX buffer in the I2C driver. The `Wire` instance in user app is separated instance and if user specify a larger buffer (>32 bytes) for the driver, the I2C will be deinitialized, which may results in corrupting the operation to PMIC and FuelGauge in the system power manager. This is fixed by https://github.com/particle-iot/device-os/pull/2890. The PR herein reverts some of those changes and fix the issue from another perspective.
2. (Gen4 only) `hal_i2c_transaction()` doesn't check if the I2C is enabled before going forward.
3. If the I2C interface that is used by system power manager is ended by `Wire.end()` in user app, the system power manager may return unexpected state.

### Solution
1. (Gen4 only) I2C is not disabled in `deInit()`, we should not change the enable state, but change the configured state only.
2. Checks I2C enable state in `hal_i2c_transaction()`
3. Introduce HAL APIs to make sure the I2C interface used by system power manager is not diabled by user app.

### Example App
```cpp
