### Problem

The current mechanism for UART data receiving is depending on the `NRF_UARTE_EVENT_ENDRX` event and a timer counter that is connected with the `NRF_UARTE_EVENT_RXDRDY` event through PPI. When the `NRF_UARTE_EVENT_ENDRX` is asserted, the receiver is stopped automatically by hardware. Everytime we service the `NRF_UARTE_EVENT_ENDRX` we restart the UART receiver. Only if the receiver is started, the data we received can be moved into the RAM buffer. If we query the data when UART is receiving data, the timer can tell us how many bytes are received. But note that when `NRF_UARTE_EVENT_RXDRDY` is generated, it doesn't mean that the received byte has been moved to the RAM buffer.

<img width="819" alt="Screenshot 2023-08-15 at 15 40 53" src="https://github.com/particle-iot/device-os/assets/8130559/de24ce04-25ea-4358-a91d-36c7995b46a2">

The problem of the current mechanism is if the `NRF_UARTE_EVENT_ENDRX` event is postponed to deal with by, for example, other interrupts with higher priority, while UART is receiving data, then the `NRF_UARTE_EVENT_RXDRDY` is asserted, but the receiver has stopped and the received byte cannot be moved to the RAM buffer, and we lost the byte. The longer the `NRF_UARTE_EVENT_ENDRX` being postponed, the more data will lose. This happens at the middle of a data receiving frame when the `NRF_UARTE_EVENT_ENDRX` is asserted, so using a larger buffer doesn't fix the issue.

-> incoming data stream:  00 01 02 03 04 05 06 07 08 09 ...
-> 1. rx buffer  [ (free spaces) | (acquired length = 5) 00 01 02 03 04 ] <- NRF_UARTE_EVENT_ENDRX, 05 06 07 ... may be lost
-> 2. rx buffer  [ (free spaces) | (acquired length = 10000,  commited = 9995) 00 01 02 03 04 ] <- NRF_UARTE_EVENT_ENDRX, 05 06 07 ... may be lost

The above is the typical circumstance how data is lost. Sometimes we don't lose data, but we may break a data frame. For example, Normally the `NRF_UARTE_EVENT_ENDRX` is generated before the `NRF_UARTE_EVENT_RXDRDY` of the byte `N` as shown in the attached screenshot. If the `NRF_UARTE_EVENT_ENDRX` is not services in time, the `NRF_UARTE_EVENT_RXDRDY` will increase the timer counter by 1, followed by servicing the `NRF_UARTE_EVENT_ENDRX` event, in which case the timer counter is cleared. In this case, we did received the byte N and the byte `N` can be moved to the RAM buffer once we restart the receiver in ISR, but since the timer counter is cleared, we will start counting the received data with byte `N+1`.

1. Assume we are going to receive contiguous 43 bytes
2. When the 42th byte is received, the `NRF_UARTE_EVENT_ENDRX` is serviced and timer counter is cleared, but the 43th byte is ready to be moved to the RAM buffer. Since user keep querying and read the received data, `available()` is now `0`.
3. Assume the 2nd transaction will acquire `10` bytes from the RX ring buffer.
4. Receiver restarted and the 43th byte is moved to the RAM buffer, but `available()` is still `0`. The first 43-bytes transaction timeout
5. 2nd 43-bytes is receiving. When the next `NRF_UARTE_EVENT_ENDRX` is asserted, there is `10` bytes in the ring buffer, but the timer counter is `9`, since the timer start counting when the first byte of the 2nd transaction is received. Now the `available()` is `10`, since we `rxBuffer_.acquireCommit(rxBuffer_.acquirePending());`(pending is 1) when we handle the `NRF_UARTE_EVENT_ENDRX` event.
6. Assume we will acquire `40` bytes from the RX ring buffer. Now there is still 34 bytes to be received for the 2nd transaction.
7. The timer counter increases by 34. The total `available()` since step5 is 44.
8. So the 1st transaction we received 42 bytes and the 2nd transaction we receved 44 bytes. 

### Solution

1. Use double buffering and enable `NRF_UARTE_SHORT_ENDRX_STARTRX` shortcut and the `NRF_UARTE_INT_RXSTARTED_MASK`, by doing this the receiver will automatically started when it detected the `NRF_UARTE_EVENT_ENDRX` and we prepare the next buffer for the next transaction when the current receiver started.

2. Do not clear the timer counter. The timer is always counting and there is a variable for tracking how many bytes are commited. It doesn't matter even if the timer overflows. We can easily handle this situation by comparing the counter value and the variable.

### Step To Test

1. Flash the below examples to two gen3 devices.
2. Connect these two devices over the Serial1 interface.
3. Connect the USB serial to start monitoring the test result.

