### Problem
The tracker/trackerm platforms communicate with a handful of i2c devices on system startup. The system allocates and provides the default 32 byte sized I2C Wire buffers to the I2C HAL. The I2C hal is able to communicate and initialize the peripherals, all is well.

However, we have a new Quectel GNSS device that returns data longer than 32 bytes in its I2C responses. 

Normally, a user can allocate their own larger I2C buffers via defining a `acquireWireBuffer()` function. However, when the user app constructs its own `TwoWire` object and attempts to re-initialize the i2c HAL, the system code rejects it (in the case of the NRF hal), since it has already been initialized. 

see [SC-109914](https://app.shortcut.com/particle/story/109914/allow-user-app-to-override-i2c-buffer-on-tracker-trackerm-other-platforms-that-may-have-rtc-and-pmic) for details
### Solution

There should be 2 changes:
1. The default system i2c buffer should be increased for the tracker/trackerm platforms in order to allow the system to read any data it wishes from the GNSS device
2. The `acquireWireBuffer()` function should work on any platform, even if the system has allocated buffers already. The only caveat to this is that if a user wishes to override the system buffers, they must be larger than the currently allocated ones. 

### Steps to Test

Run example app

### Example App

```
SerialLogHandler g_logSerial(LOG_LEVEL_ALL);

hal_i2c_config_t allocateWireConfig(uint32_t bufferSize) {
    hal_i2c_config_t config = {
        .size = sizeof(hal_i2c_config_t),
        .version = HAL_I2C_CONFIG_VERSION_1,
        .rx_buffer = new (std::nothrow) uint8_t[bufferSize],
        .rx_buffer_size = bufferSize,
        .tx_buffer = new (std::nothrow) uint8_t[bufferSize],
        .tx_buffer_size = bufferSize,
        .flags = HAL_I2C_CONFIG_FLAG_FREEABLE
    };
    return config;
}

void setup() {
  Wire.begin();
}

void loop() {

  hal_i2c_config_t biggerBuffer = allocateWireConfig(128);
  int reinitLargerResult = hal_i2c_init(HAL_I2C_INTERFACE1, &biggerBuffer);
  Log.info("I2C Hal Reinit bigger buffer result %d", reinitLargerResult);

  hal_i2c_config_t smallerBuffer = allocateWireConfig(10);
  int reinitSmallerResult = hal_i2c_init(HAL_I2C_INTERFACE1, &smallerBuffer);
  Log.info("I2C Hal Reinit smaller buffer result %d", reinitSmallerResult);

 delay(3000);
}
```

The first `hal_i2c_init()` call should succeed, as it is increasing the default i2c buffer from 32 -> 128
The second `hal_i2c_init()` call should fail, as it is trying to shrink the i2c buffer from 128 -> 10
Something like this:
```
0000009039 [app] INFO: I2C Hal Reinit bigger buffer result 0
0000009041 [app] INFO: I2C Hal Reinit smaller buffer result -191
```

