Some applications wish to manage their own battery model using the Maxim fuel gauge part included with some device platforms.  In order to allow the application and  DVOS to stay in sync, this PR provides a way for the user app to inform DVOS as to which bits precision (18 or 19) should be used when calculating the State of Charge.  (By default the current FuelGauge implementation uses 18 bits, so unless your app requires 19 bits, there is no reason to use this call.)

This can be used by eg:

```'

void setup() {
    FuelGauge fg(true);
    fg.setCustomSoCPrecision(19);
}

void loop() {
    static uint32_t loop_count = 0;

    FuelGauge fg(true);
    float soc = fg.getSoC();
    float nsoc = fg.getNormalizedSoC();
    Log.info("loop: %lu soc19:  %0.2f nsoc19: %0.2f", loop_count, soc, nsoc);

    delay(3000);
    loop_count++;
}
```

Note that this change is limited in scope to the current Maxim fuel gauge driver, and only applicable on device platforms that use this driver. This is not more broadly applicable to any generalized fuel gauge class we may release in the future.

# BLE provisioning mode

Provisioning mode allows the device to enter BLE setup process. When the device enters provisioning mode, it should be able to connect and pair with the mobile app. The module's cloud connectivity does not get disrupted while entering / in provisioning mode. The provisioning mode may run-side-by-side at all times with the user application. The user app must use `SYSTEM_THREAD(ENABLED)` but may use any of the app modes: `MANUAL`, `SEMI_AUTOMATIC`, `AUTOMATIC`. Provisioning mode and its APIs do not work when listening mode is enabled
This #2379 PR contains the changes to use the flag to disable listening mode

### CUSTOM SERVICE AND CHARACTERISTIC UUIDs
- Provisioning mode allows the device to connect using custom service and characteristic UUIDs instead of the [default ones](https://github.com/particle-iot/device-os/blob/develop/system/src/ble_control_request_channel.cpp#L95-L105)
- The custom UUIDs must be set before entering provisioning mode, and can be set only once throughout the runtime of the program (**TODO**: Investigating whether dynamic re-config of UUIDs is possible)
- Provisioning mode BLE coexists with other BLE advertising a user app is doing, within the resource constraints of the device. The resource constraints include the size of the advertisement packet, dynamic reconfiguration of the UUIDs to name a few
- Provisioning custom UUIDs will stick even after provisioning mode has exited

### SETUP CODE
- BLE module advertises setup code in place of manufacturing data when the device is in provisioning mode
- When module exits provisioning mode, the default manufacturing data is advertised
- This may not be overwritten in provisioning mode but multiple advertising data can be displayed simultaneously in provisioning mode

### CUSTOM MOBILE SECRET
- Supports setting a custom mobile secret through HAL API which is exposed to the user app through dynalib
- The custom mobile secret gets written into DCT
- Supports clearing the custom mobile secret through HAL API to restore the mobile secret that is programmed during manufacturing

### CUSTOM COMPANY ID
- Company ID used for advertising can be modified. Takes a 16-bit value
- If this API is not used, Particle company ID is used

### SYSTEM EVENTS
- Provides system events for the following
    - BLE connected / disconnected
    - Handshake done / failed / error
    - Other events such as network credentials set/clear are handled through the network_credentials system events

### DEVICE NAME
- Device name set by the user sticks throughout provisioning mode. (Device name previously would revert to default name ARGON-XXXXXX when listening mode was entered)

### CONTROL REQUEST FILTER
Processes control requests that are input through a vector. The filter can be cleared through `System.clearControlRequestFilter()` where all the requests are processed. Inputs are obtained through `system_control_req_filter` from the user app which contains the requests to be executed. 
```
