### Problem

As a firmware developer I often need to split a string on a delimiter like when I receive an event I subscribed to. Splitting a String is not documented in our firmware docs and using the C function `strtok` is difficult because it modifies the actual string.

### Solution

Add an easy to use Wiring API `String::split(delimiter)`

It ends up doing 2 copies of the string in the process. Since `strtok_r` modifies the original buffer the whole string is first copied to keep `String::split` a `const` method. Then the `next()` method copies the C string back into a `String` before returning it.

### Steps to Test

Compilation API and unit tests included

Run `firmware/user/tests/unit$ make`

Note that I had to fix `services/inc/dcd.h` to make the unit tests compile. I didn't include it in this PR because it was unrelated and it's easy to do in a separate PR.
```
		bool isValid() const {
			return watermark==WATERMARK;
		}
```

I also had to comment out the last 2 unit tests from `user/tests/unit/string_interpolate.cpp` because they were segfaulting the unit tests on my machine before I added my new code.

### Example App

```c
#include "Particle.h"

void setup() {
  Serial.begin();
  String data("D7=HIGH");
  auto parts = data.split("=");
  String name = parts.next();
  if (!parts.hasNext()) {
    Serial.println("Couldn't find =");
    return;
  }
  String value = parts.next();
  Serial.printlnf("name=" + name + " value=" + value);
}
```

