### Changes
- Include HAL thread functions in dynalib
- Allow lambdas as thread functions
- Support periodic tasks with the helper class PeriodicWork, which delegates to vTaskDelayUntil to ensure constant execution period
- Add simple CriticalSection concurrency primitive
- Removed ThreadFactory because `thread t = Thread.create(...)` is different than other Wiring APIs
### Motivation

The Particle platform is missing a straightforward way to service hardware at a regular interval in a predictable fashion with absolutely no possibility of blocking. Adding lightweight Thread support to Wiring allows this. PeriodicWork ensures that periods will be accurate, especially for short periods (< 5ms).

We've been having lots of discussions in #614 about blocking/non-blocking calls. Even with the multi-threading approach many calls will block for unpredictably long periods of time. I've been thinking about this a lot and rather than fix the blocking, I think it will be easier to provide a way to deal with the physical world in its own reserved thread.
### Approach

 Wiring threads are complementary to multithreading. It's possible to use either or both.

Personally with Wiring threads I don't think my applications will need SYSTEM_THREAD(ENABLED). I'll just leave all the blocking stuff together in setup() and loop() and service the hardware in its own thread.

It will be up to developers to only use appropriate calls in their threads (GPIO, I2C, SPI, etc) and make sure multiple threads don't use the same peripherals. Cloud calls should be made on the application thread.

For concurrency control, I think a simple CriticalSection will go a long way, but this is the weakest area of this PR. More concurrency primitives could be added later if they are needed.
### (Contrived) Example

``` c++
#include "application.h"
void setup()
{
    RGB.control(true);

    Thread hardwareThread = Thread("hardware", [&]() {
        // 100ms periodic task
        PeriodicWork work(100);

        bool state = false;
        for(int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
            work.start();
            RGB.color(0, 0, state ? 180 : 0);
            state = !state;
            // Will sleep for 100ms from the call to work.start()
            work.end();
        }
        // a task should never return, but delete itself when it is done
        // but most tasks will live forever anyway
        hardwareThread.dispose();
    }, OS_THREAD_PRIORITY_DEFAULT + 1);

    // join should be as rare as dispose
    hardwareThread.join();
    RGB.color(0, 255, 0);
}
```
